1. PERMISSION IS HEREBY GRANTED, FREE OF CHARGE, TO ANY PERSON OBTAINING A COPY

Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy of the
Software defined in Section 2, to deal in that Software as provided in Sections 3
and 4, subject to the notice condition in Section 5 and with the disclaimers in
Sections 6 and 7.

The grant takes effect as a permission in the covered material within the rights the
grantor is entitled to license. It is not conditional on an application,
registration, favorable purpose, noncommercial status, payment to the grantor, or
exercise of every permitted activity. Definitions and scenarios specify the
construction of this grant; they do not establish additional recipient duties.

References to the Software before the completion of its definition refer to the
definition in Section 2. Forward references do not defer or suspend the grant until
a reader has performed a separate administrative act.

1.1 — Meaning and extent of "Person"

A reference to a person obtaining a copy is not restricted by occupation, residence,
nationality, organizational role, source of funding, or whether the recipient acts
in a personal or professional capacity.

Where the law recognizes an entity as capable of receiving permission, the
descriptive use of person does not exclude that entity merely because it is an
organization rather than a natural human individual. Its legal capacity remains a
matter of applicable law.

1.2 — Meaning and extent of "Obtaining"

Obtaining describes receiving or acquiring a copy through a means that makes the
licensed material available to the recipient. The grant does not require a purchase,
a particular download page, a personal introduction to the author, or a written
statement of intended use.

The license grants rights in the Software; it does not independently authorize
access to someone else's account, computer, storage device, or restricted service.
Permission to use a work and permission to access a particular system are distinct
questions.

1.3 — Meaning and extent of "Copy"

A copy is a reproduction of licensed material in a form relevant to the applicable
legal and factual circumstances. A file extension, a filename, a storage path, or a
compression method does not by itself determine whether material is a copy.

This license does not supply a universal legal test for reproduction. The same
sequence of bytes may be stored, encoded, transmitted, or reconstructed using
different mechanisms without the mechanism itself becoming an additional license
condition.

1.4 — Meaning and extent of "Free of charge"

Free of charge describes the permission granted by the licensor without a license
fee under the provisions of this license. It does not imply that all infrastructure,
hardware, hosting, or labor involved in a use has no cost.

Costs charged by a distributor or service provider do not automatically revoke the
original permission. The right to sell copies is expressly included in this license.

1.5 — Meaning and extent of "Author"

Author refers to a person or persons associated with authorship as relevant to the
disclaimer herein and applicable law. The label does not promise that only one
person contributed to the Software.

Authorship and ownership can differ. This license does not assign another person's
copyright, resolve employment ownership, or replace the permission needed for
material that its distributor has no authority to license.

1.6 — Meaning and extent of "Copyright holder"

A copyright holder is a person or entity holding applicable copyright interests. A
maintainer, uploader, sponsor, distributor, or repository administrator is not
necessarily the holder of every interest in every component.

This license grants permission within the rights held or validly licensed by its
grantor. This license is not a mechanism for enlarging those underlying rights by
definition.

1.T1 — Meaning of "hereby"

The word hereby identifies this license as the instrument through which the stated
permission is granted. It does not denote a separate ceremony, a personal
communication to each recipient, or a future document that must be obtained before
the permission described herein can be exercised.

The effective application of the grant depends on this license being applied to the
covered material by a person authorized to grant the relevant rights. The occurrence
of hereby in a document cannot itself establish ownership, authority, or the legal
effectiveness of an unauthorized grant.

1.T2 — Time of writing, publication, receipt, and reading

Writing, publishing, receiving, storing, and reading a license are distinguishable
events. The grant does not impose a requirement that those events occur
simultaneously, in a prescribed order apart from what is inherent in the facts, or
on a particular calendar date.

The absence of evidence that the recipient read the document at a specified instant
does not establish a reading-time restriction that this license does not state.
Whether a particular arrangement creates an enforceable permission or agreement
remains subject to applicable law.

1.T3 — Delayed and repeated reading

A later reading does not constitute an application for a newly priced grant. A
repeated reading does not multiply permission fees, create a new recipient identity,
or impose a repeated notice ceremony. Reading in several sittings is not treated as
several separate grants.

These descriptions do not determine legal questions concerning notice or acceptance
where those questions arise under applicable law. They specify that the grant
contains no separate scheduling condition for reading the instrument.

1.T4 — Meaning of "any person"

Any person means each recipient within the class contemplated by the grant, not a
single person to be selected by the grantor after distribution. Exercise of
permission by one recipient does not consume an allotment of permissions available
to others.

The phrase does not obligate a recipient to identify all other possible recipients,
deliver copies to them, collect their signatures, obtain their approval, or notify
every person on Earth that a grant exists.

1.T5 — Individual and collective recipients

A reference to any person does not make the permissions mutually exclusive among
persons. Several recipients may hold copies at the same time and may exercise
permitted activities independently or collaboratively within their relevant rights.

A group is not automatically an independent legal person merely because its members
share a project. The grant does not require the creation of a new legal entity, and
applicable law continues to determine the capacity and responsibilities of actual
participants.

1.T6 — Quantifiers do not create technical capacity

A grant to any recipient and a permission concerning covered copies do not promise
unlimited computing capacity, continuous access to a download service, unlimited
physical inventory, or the performance of services by the licensor.

Universality within the described permission category is distinct from an
undertaking to make every possible exercise practically achievable. A lack of
available resources is not itself a new license prohibition.

2. OF THIS SOFTWARE AND ASSOCIATED DOCUMENTATION FILES (THE "SOFTWARE")

For the purposes of this license, "Software" means the software and associated
documentation files to which the copyright holder applies this license. The
definition identifies covered material rather than assigning every item in the
recipient's environment to the same license.

The definition applies to covered material notwithstanding changes of
representation, packaging, storage, naming, or arrangement. The descriptions below
specify those concepts without enlarging the copyright holder's underlying rights,
licensing separately owned material, or imposing an obligation to disclose other
material.

The absence of a technical representation from the following descriptions does not
exclude it from the grant when it is otherwise covered material.

2.1 — Meaning and extent of "Software"

The expression defined as "Software" in this license refers to the software and
associated documentation files to which that grant applies. It is not a reference to
every item stored on the same computer, every dependency that can interact with that
software, or every work produced by its users.

A package may contain separately licensed material. Physical proximity, a common
archive, a common repository, or a common installer does not confer rights that the
applicable copyright holder has not granted. This observation describes the boundary
of the grant rather than imposing a new duty to investigate every unrelated file.

2.2 — Meaning and extent of "Source code"

Source code describes a representation ordinarily used for understanding or changing
a program. It may include human-authored text, generated text, macro definitions,
templates, build instructions, or other project material where those materials form
part of the licensed Software.

Whether an item qualifies as source code under a particular statute or technical
convention does not introduce a source-disclosure obligation. This license does not
condition distribution on supplying a preferred form for modification.

2.3 — Meaning and extent of "Object code"

Object code describes a representation produced or used at a stage of translation,
assembly, linking, loading, or execution. The description does not require that the
representation be directly executable without additional software or processing.

Object code is not an exemption from the notice provision merely because its
contents are inconvenient for a person to read. Conversely, the description is not
an instruction to alter every object file or embed a duplicate notice in every
machine instruction.

2.4 — Meaning and extent of "Executable"

An executable is a representation capable of being executed in its relevant
environment, whether natively, through an interpreter, through a virtual machine, or
after an ordinary loading or translation step.

The presence or absence of an .exe suffix does not create a different permission
category. A shared library, a script, a firmware image, and a bytecode module may
involve the same licensed material under different technical arrangements.

2.5 — Meaning and extent of "File"

A file is a technical unit in which information is represented or accessed. Its
boundaries can be selected by tooling and do not necessarily coincide with the
boundaries of an original work, a modification, or a substantial portion of that
work.

Splitting a work into many files does not by itself remove the notice condition.
Combining many works into one file does not by itself make all of them subject to a
grant given by only one copyright holder.

2.6 — Meaning and extent of "Directory"

A directory is an organizational arrangement of files or references to files. Its
name, nesting depth, capitalization, or location does not by itself expand or narrow
the grant stated herein.

A directory called proprietary, public, private, vendor, third_party, or anything
else is not a substitute for the applicable license. Moving licensed material
between directories does not constitute a separately chargeable permission event.

2.7 — Meaning and extent of "Repository"

A repository is an arrangement used to maintain or distribute project material and
may include history, branches, tags, metadata, and snapshots. A repository
identifier is not a mandatory label that all derivatives must retain.

The grant concerns licensed material. It does not automatically license every
contribution, attachment, issue comment, or external resource merely because a
hosting platform displays that resource beside the repository.

2.8 — Meaning and extent of "Archive"

An archive is a container or representation used to collect, compress, encode, or
transport material. The archive may contain one file, many files, nested archives,
or references to other resources.

Using a ZIP file, a tar file, an installer, an image, or another container does not
introduce a separate permission requirement. The obligation to preserve notices is
evaluated with respect to the licensed material being distributed rather than the
marketing name of the container.

2.9 — Meaning and extent of "Medium"

A medium is a means of carrying or storing information. It may be physical,
electronic, temporary, persistent, local, remote, or composed of several technical
layers.

This explanatory classification does not attempt to decide which temporary technical
events are legally relevant copies. It establishes only that the grant herein does
not expressly reserve permission to a favored storage technology.

2.10 — Meaning and extent of "Memory"

Memory describes an information-storage facility used by a computing system,
including facilities commonly described as volatile or nonvolatile. Information can
occupy memory without becoming a separately authored work.

The license does not require an application to display a notice after each
allocation, cache fill, page fault, or movement of bytes. Technical repetition is
not converted here into a requirement for repetitive user-facing notification.

2.11 — Meaning and extent of "Transmission"

Transmission describes movement or availability of information through a
communication mechanism. It can include successive stages, intermediaries,
encodings, and buffering.

A transmission mechanism does not by itself identify which participant performs a
legally relevant distribution. The grant stated herein permits distribution subject
to its notice condition, while applicable law determines the significance of the
particular activity.

2.12 — Meaning and extent of "Associated documentation"

Associated documentation files are included within the definition herein of Software
when covered by the license. Manuals, examples, comments, and build instructions may
serve documentary functions without being executable.

A document need not be named manual or documentation to be associated documentation.
An external document is not automatically covered merely because a licensed document
links to it.

2.13 — Meaning and extent of "Substantial portion"

Substantial portion is the expression used by this license and is not replaced here
by a byte count, a percentage, a line count, or a test based on whether a file is
individually executable.

Substantiality and copyright protection can depend on qualitative as well as
quantitative considerations. This license does not declare that a fragment below an
invented numerical threshold is always exempt from notice requirements.

2.14 — Meaning and extent of "Whole"

The whole of the Software describes the licensed material considered together. It
does not automatically encompass unrelated operating systems, compilers, services,
applications, or hardware needed to process that material.

Describing an installation as a whole product does not transfer rights in its
separately owned components. The grant can apply to a component even when that
component is marketed as part of a larger product.

2.15 — Meaning and extent of "Portion"

A portion is some part of the licensed material. Selection may occur by editing,
extraction, compilation, rearrangement, or another technical operation rather than
by copying an entire original file.

The notice condition expressly addresses substantial portions. This definition does
not expand that condition to every imaginable resemblance, shared idea, common word,
or trivial fragment.

2.16 — Meaning and extent of "Derivative"

A derivative in this license is a descriptive reference to modified or adapted
material where relevant. The applicable legal definition of a derivative work is not
established solely by a filename, a project label, or the recipient's choice of
terminology.

Permission to modify and merge is broad, but this license does not claim that every
output of a licensed program is necessarily derived from that program. Copyright in
output depends on what protected material, if any, is actually involved.

2.17 — Meaning and extent of "Compilation"

Compilation describes transformation of program material into another
representation. Preprocessing, optimization, assembly, and linking may participate
in that process or be performed as distinct stages.

Compilation does not require the original author's chosen compiler, flags, platform,
optimization level, or version. This license supplies permission, not a guarantee
that every compiler invocation will succeed or preserve intended behavior.

2.18 — Meaning and extent of "Generated material"

Generated material is material produced through a tool or automated process. The
fact of generation does not by itself determine authorship, copyright protection,
provenance, or applicable licensing.

If generated output includes protected material covered by this license, the grant
and notice condition remain relevant. If output contains no such material, this
license does not apply to it merely because a tool covered by this license
participated in its creation.

2.19 — Meaning and extent of "Interface"

An interface is a point or convention through which components interact. An
interface can be described by function signatures, messages, files, protocols, or
other technical arrangements.

Permission to use the Software does not require a recipient to preserve an
interface. Conversely, changing an interface does not guarantee that other
components will remain compatible or that separate rights governing them cease to
apply.

2.20 — Meaning and extent of "Identity"

Identity refers to distinguishing a work, version, component, or participant in
context. Renaming a work is not, by itself, removal of its provenance or elimination
of its applicable notices.

The grant stated herein does not require derivatives to retain the original project
name. Permissions under copyright do not automatically supply trademark rights or a
right to misrepresent affiliation.

2.21 — Meaning and extent of "Version"

A version is an identified state of material. Its identifier may be a number, a
date, a tag, a commit, a hash, a label, or no explicit designation at all.

A version label does not by itself determine the license applicable to every
included component. Updating this license cannot simply rewrite the terms under
which a person already obtained an earlier licensed copy.

2.T1 — Copies of copies

A reproduction can be made from another reproduction rather than from the first file
published by the grantor. The fact that a source copy was obtained through an
intermediary does not by itself remove the grant from otherwise covered material or
introduce an upstream-contact requirement.

A chain of copying does not convey rights absent from the applicable grant. Material
added by intermediaries, separately licensed components, and technical access
restrictions are not automatically governed by this grant solely because they occupy
positions in the same chain.

2.T2 — Generation numbers

First-generation, second-generation, subsequent-generation, and descendant copy are
descriptive labels for provenance. A generation count is not a royalty multiplier,
an expiration counter, a maximum number of authorized transfers, or a prescribed
classification that recipients must maintain.

No intermediary can make a covered copy exempt from the notice condition solely by
assigning a high generation number. No recipient is required by this license to
produce a complete ancestry chart before exercising a granted permission.

2.T3 — Fidelity and transformation

A copy may preserve bytes exactly or represent covered material through compression,
translation, compilation, rearrangement, or another permitted transformation.
Whether the result contains protected licensed material is considered according to
its content and applicable law rather than solely its distance from the original
byte sequence.

The definition does not treat every program with similar behavior as a copy.
Independently expressed material, unprotected ideas, commonplace elements, and
unrelated output are not brought within the notice condition merely by a claim of
conceptual resemblance.

2.T4 — Forks, mirrors, and recovery

A mirror, a fork, a restored backup, and a reconstructed checkout may contain
covered copies even when the original hosting service is unavailable. Those
descriptions are not mutually exclusive classes and are not separate permission
tiers.

Copying permissions do not authenticate a mirror or promise that a restored copy is
complete, unmodified, trustworthy, executable, or free of separately introduced
material. Authority and technical fidelity are not guaranteed by the terminology.

2.T5 — Many copies and one transaction

One transaction can furnish many copies, and many technical operations can
participate in furnishing one packaged copy. The relationship between transaction
count and copy count is not specified as a fee schedule or additional notice
ceremony.

The notice condition continues to concern all copies or substantial portions as
specified in Section 5, including those considered in distribution examples. This
clarification does not create a blanket private-copy exemption, impose a user-facing
ceremony for every CPU movement of a byte, or exempt a delivery containing multiple
covered copies.

2.T6 — A copy as evidence

Possession of a file can be relevant to the factual question of what material a
recipient has obtained. Possession alone does not conclusively resolve authorship,
authenticity, authority to grant rights, satisfaction of access restrictions, or
every dispute about licensing history.

This license does not require recipients to maintain notarized copying logs,
recorded download sessions, provenance diagrams, or certificates of generational
fidelity. It also does not prevent otherwise applicable law from assigning
evidentiary significance to relevant records.

3. TO DEAL IN THE SOFTWARE WITHOUT RESTRICTION, INCLUDING WITHOUT LIMITATION

The recipient may deal in the Software without restriction within the granted
rights, subject to the notice condition expressly stated in Section 5. The
enumeration in Section 4 is inclusive and not exhaustive. Neither a missing example
nor a difference in technical vocabulary reserves an additional permission to the
grantor.

This license does not confer rights the grantor does not possess, authorize access
to another person's systems, or override mandatory law. These boundaries distinguish
the grant from unrelated legal questions; they do not impose a new test of whether
the grantor approves a recipient or its purpose.

The following construction provisions apply to every listed permission, every
combination of those permissions, and dealings not individually named in the
enumeration.

3.1 — Meaning and extent of "Other dealings"

Other dealings is the broad residual expression used by this license. It prevents
the listed verbs from functioning as an exclusive set of permitted technical
operations.

The expression is not an invitation to override applicable law or acquire rights the
grantor does not possess. It also does not require the recipient to obtain a new
permission whenever an operation lacks a dedicated heading in this license.

3.2 — Meaning and extent of "Example"

An example illustrates a reading of the grant stated herein or condition. It is not
an additional contract, an exhaustive definition, or a separate undertaking to
perform the illustrated act.

Failure of a real case to match an example exactly does not mean that the real case
is forbidden. Examples do not take precedence over the provisions of this license.

3.3 — Meaning and extent of "Including"

Including introduces members of a category without necessarily exhausting it. In the
grant stated herein, including without limitation expressly indicates that the
listed rights do not form a closed list.

An additional example may illuminate an unlisted instance. It does not create a new
requirement to establish analogy to a previously printed scenario before permission
applies.

3.4 — Meaning and extent of "Without restriction"

Without restriction describes the breadth of the permission granted herein subject
to the condition stated in this license. It is not read here as deleting that
condition or overriding law outside the grant.

Neither this expression nor an explanatory example grants access to confidential
systems, assigns another person's work, promises service availability, or waives
third-party rights.

3.5 — Meaning and extent of "Condition"

Condition refers here to the notice condition expressly stated in this license. A
technical prerequisite, explanatory qualification, or illustrative sequence in this
license is not thereby a new license condition.

The words must or shall, if used when describing a computer's behavior or applicable
law, do not create an additional recipient obligation under this license. The
principal provisions control its interpretation.

3.6 — Meaning and extent of "And/or"

And/or expresses that listed activities can be performed separately or in
combination. Permission to sell does not require first publishing, and permission to
modify does not require subsequently selling.

The grant does not impose a prescribed workflow through its sequence of verbs.
Different recipients may exercise different subsets of the permitted rights.

3.C1 — Construction concerning: Must a recipient send a copy of its changes upstream

No such requirement appears in this license. The permission to modify is not
conditioned on contribution.

3.C2 — Construction concerning: Must a recipient disclose its application source

This license does not impose a general source-disclosure requirement. Other
applicable licenses or separate agreements can present different questions.

3.C3 — Construction concerning: Must a recipient sell at zero price

No. Free of charge describes the original grant, while the grant expressly permits
selling copies.

3.C4 — Construction concerning: Must a recipient retain the original project name

This license does not impose that requirement. Preserving an applicable copyright
notice is a different matter.

3.C5 — Construction concerning: Must a recipient use the original build system

No such restriction appears in this license. Technical feasibility and permission
are distinct.

3.C6 — Construction concerning: Must a recipient obtain approval for commercial use

This license does not reserve commercial use for separately approved recipients.

3.C7 — Construction concerning: Must a recipient disclose its reasons for using the
Software

This license does not require a declaration of motive or an application for
permission.

3.C8 — Construction concerning: Must a recipient use every listed right

No. The rights can be exercised separately or in combination.

3.C9 — Construction concerning: Does a defect cancel permission

This license does not make the absence of defects a prerequisite to permission.

3.C10 — Construction concerning: Does permission guarantee a right to an account or
service

No. The grant in the Software does not independently grant access to another party's
systems or services.

3.C12 — Construction concerning: Does the existence of this license certify legal
compliance

No. It is a license with elaborated provisions, not a certification or professional
legal opinion.

3.C13 — Construction concerning: Does the grant include every unrelated work in a
package

No. Rights in separately owned or separately licensed material require their own
analysis.

3.C14 — Construction concerning: Does an unlisted technical operation require
permission

Its absence from an example does not impose a permission procedure. The grant stated
herein expressly uses including without limitation.

3.C15 — Construction concerning: Does changing a filename avoid notice preservation

No such exception follows from a filename change. The notice condition herein
concerns copies and substantial portions.

3.T1 — Any, all, and every

A reference to any member of a category leaves no additional licensor-selected
subset within that category unless the substantive grant expressly establishes one.
A reference to all copies in the notice condition concerns the copies subject to
that condition, not all unrelated information in existence.

Every operates distributively when the context requires each covered instance to be
considered. These linguistic descriptions do not create a mandate to enumerate all
instances, prove a universal negative, or carry out an impossible inspection of
every possible future distribution.

3.T2 — Choice and conjunction

Permission to perform activity A and permission to perform activity B permit the
recipient to exercise either activity or both within their respective scopes. The
conjunction does not establish a duty to perform B as the price of exercising A
unless an express condition makes that relationship applicable.

In the enumeration of rights herein, no such requirement is established. Using
without publishing, modifying without selling, and selling without making a new
modification are not disallowed solely because the relevant verbs appear in the same
sentence.

3.T3 — Inclusive disjunction

The expression and/or in the enumeration means one or more of the specified
activities, including combinations of them. It does not require exactly one
activity, exactly two activities, or a partial performance of every activity.

The slash is a punctuation device for expressing alternatives together with their
possible combination. It is not a division operator applied to the activities, a
requirement to alternate them, or a technical instruction to divide a work into
matching proportions.

3.T4 — Order and overlap

The permitted activities may overlap in time or form part of a longer process. A
copy can be modified while a separate copy is distributed, and a merged work can be
published and sold in the same transaction.

The license does not allocate a particular time slot to each right, impose a
mutual-exclusion mechanism between rights, or require a recipient to stop one
permitted activity before beginning another. Practical dependencies remain matters
of the chosen technical arrangement.

3.T5 — Purpose of elaboration

An elaboration specifies a reading of an already stated grant, condition, or
disclaimer. It does not acquire the character of an independent restriction merely
because it contains more words, distinguishes more cases, or uses a numbered
subdivision.

Where an illustration could otherwise suggest a narrower scope than the principal
grant, the principal grant controls that illustration. Where the notice condition
applies, an illustration cannot be used to erase it.

3.T6 — Shall, may, and descriptive necessity

Shall in the notice condition states the requirement imposed by that condition. May
in a permission provision identifies an authorized option rather than an obligation
to exercise it. A description of what a compiler or protocol must do can identify
technical necessity without introducing a legal obligation to use that compiler or
protocol.

No recipient obligation is established merely by an incidental statement that a
technical event requires another technical event. The distinction between a
normative license condition and a description of technical facts is maintained
throughout this instrument.

4. THE RIGHTS TO USE, COPY, MODIFY, MERGE, PUBLISH, DISTRIBUTE, SUBLICENSE, AND/OR
SELL COPIES, AND TO PERMIT PERSONS TO WHOM THE SOFTWARE IS FURNISHED TO DO SO

The recipient is permitted to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute,
sublicense, and/or sell copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the
Software is furnished to do so. These rights may be exercised separately,
successively, concurrently, or in combinations not individually described below.

No ordering of the verbs establishes a required sequence of operations. Permission
to sell does not require preceding publication; permission to modify does not
require subsequent distribution; permission to distribute does not require
modification.

For each right, the following provisions specify its extent under several technical
and transactional arrangements. They are instances of the same grant, not separate
licenses, authorization stages, numerical limits, or exclusive categories of
recipients.

4.1 — Permission to use

A recipient may execute, inspect, evaluate, or otherwise use the Software within the
grant stated herein.

No separate upstream approval is introduced for the identity of an operator, the
intended audience, the frequency of execution, or the commercial character of the
project.

An execution need not be preceded by a new license ceremony; permission does not
promise successful execution.

4.1.D.1 — Meaning and extent of "Use"

Use encompasses dealing with the Software through activities permitted by the grant
stated herein, including execution, examination, integration, and other applicable
operations. The term does not limit permission to the purpose preferred by the
original author.

An activity need not be productive, commercially useful, educational, efficient, or
approved by the author to fall within the grant. This license does not introduce a
test of whether a use is sufficiently worthy to receive permission.

4.1.1 — Use in a single source file

For the case in which the following arrangement is present: The licensed material is
furnished as one editable text file rather than as an archive or installer.

The scope of permission in that arrangement is as follows: A recipient may execute,
inspect, evaluate, or otherwise use the Software within the grant stated herein. No
separate upstream approval is introduced for the identity of an operator, the
intended audience, the frequency of execution, or the commercial character of the
project.

With respect to its representation: The source-file boundary does not make every
independently authored statement in an accompanying project part of the licensed
work.

With respect to notices and the boundary of the granted rights: The upstream notice
can be retained as applicable source or accompanying license material; this example
does not mandate one file layout. An execution need not be preceded by a new license
ceremony; permission does not promise successful execution.

For avoidance of a contrary inference: The combination of this permission with this
context does not establish an independent obligation to perform any other listed
activity. This example is not an exclusive route to permission and does not require
an implementation to reproduce the example's technical arrangement.

4.1.2 — Use in a multiple-file source tree

For the case in which the following arrangement is present: The licensed material is
arranged across directories, include files, implementation files, and documentation.

The scope of permission in that arrangement is as follows: A recipient may execute,
inspect, evaluate, or otherwise use the Software within the grant stated herein. No
separate upstream approval is introduced for the identity of an operator, the
intended audience, the frequency of execution, or the commercial character of the
project.

With respect to its representation: Distribution through several paths does not
establish a requirement for author approval of the tree structure.

With respect to notices and the boundary of the granted rights: Notice preservation
concerns the licensed copies or substantial portions and is not replaced by naming a
directory after its author. An execution need not be preceded by a new license
ceremony; permission does not promise successful execution.

For avoidance of a contrary inference: The combination of this permission with this
context does not establish an independent obligation to perform any other listed
activity. This example is not an exclusive route to permission and does not require
an implementation to reproduce the example's technical arrangement.

4.1.3 — Use in a compiled native application

For the case in which the following arrangement is present: The licensed material
contributes to an application translated into native machine instructions.

The scope of permission in that arrangement is as follows: A recipient may execute,
inspect, evaluate, or otherwise use the Software within the grant stated herein. No
separate upstream approval is introduced for the identity of an operator, the
intended audience, the frequency of execution, or the commercial character of the
project.

With respect to its representation: Compilation does not imply that the original
author has guaranteed performance, portability, security, or lack of defects in the
executable.

With respect to notices and the boundary of the granted rights: Where copies or
substantial portions of licensed material are distributed in compiled form, the
notice condition still matters; no compiled-distribution exception is introduced
here. An execution need not be preceded by a new license ceremony; permission does
not promise successful execution.

For avoidance of a contrary inference: The combination of this permission with this
context does not establish an independent obligation to perform any other listed
activity. This example is not an exclusive route to permission and does not require
an implementation to reproduce the example's technical arrangement.

4.1.4 — Use in a shared-library component

For the case in which the following arrangement is present: The licensed material is
delivered in a component that can be loaded by another application.

The scope of permission in that arrangement is as follows: A recipient may execute,
inspect, evaluate, or otherwise use the Software within the grant stated herein. No
separate upstream approval is introduced for the identity of an operator, the
intended audience, the frequency of execution, or the commercial character of the
project.

With respect to its representation: Dynamic loading does not impose a requirement
under this license that the calling application disclose its own source or use the
same license.

