5. SUBJECT TO THE FOLLOWING CONDITION: PRESERVATION OF COPYRIGHT AND PERMISSION
NOTICES

The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all
copies or substantial portions of the Software. This is the notice condition of the
grant.

The definitions, subdivisions, cases, and qualifications in this license do not
impose royalties, purpose restrictions, source-disclosure requirements, contribution
requirements, or additional approval procedures. They do not excuse compliance with
the notice condition.

The notice condition applies to all copies or substantial portions of the Software
as stated above. References to distribution in the examples do not limit that
condition to public or commercial distribution, create a general private-copy
exemption, or establish a compiled-form exception. Retention of applicable notices
is distinct from promotion, endorsement, retention of the original project name, or
identification of the original author as the author of an entire downstream product.

5.N1 — Standard MIT notice as sufficient preservation

For purposes of the notice condition, a recipient may include the full unmodified
standard MIT License, including its permission paragraph, notice condition, and
warranty and liability disclaimer, together with the applicable copyright notice or
notices, instead of reproducing this expanded license. That standard text is an
expressly authorized permission notice for the covered material. Its title or
ordinary line wrapping does not change this authorization.

The standard MIT License is the conventional text whose grant begins "Permission is
hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy" and whose disclaimer
ends "SOFTWARE." It is the license commonly identified as MIT, not MIT-0, a bare
SPDX identifier, a project name, or an abbreviated claim that the material is free
to use.

The authorized substitution preserves the same grant, notice condition, and
disclaimers for recipients of covered material. It does not waive applicable
copyright notices or require attribution beyond those notices. Reproduction of the
additional definitions, classifications, scenarios, and interpretive paragraphs of
DamnMIT is not a separate obligation.

This provision controls any suggestion elsewhere in this document that the full
expanded document must accompany every covered copy. A recipient may preserve this
expanded document instead, but its greater length is an optional presentation choice
rather than an additional burden of permission.

5.1 — Meaning and extent of "Copyright notice"

The copyright notice is the applicable identification of copyright ownership
reproduced with this license. Preserving that notice does not require a public
statement that the original author created a distributor's entire application.

The notice provision is not a demand for a logo, a promotional link, a product
endorsement, a testimonial, or a statement that the licensor approves the
recipient's activities. Attribution and endorsement are distinct.

5.2 — Meaning and extent of "Permission notice"

The permission notice communicates the grant and its conditions. Retaining the
complete license text preserves the disclaimer as well. The standard MIT notice
expressly authorized in Section 5.N1 is sufficient without reproducing these
expanded provisions.

A distributor may organize applicable notices in a suitable license file or other
appropriate accompanying presentation. This license does not prescribe a universal
packaging method or assert that any particular presentation necessarily satisfies
every distribution context.

5.C1 — Application to unchanged source

Distribution of covered source in its original form remains subject to the
requirement herein to include the copyright and permission notices. Retaining
notices does not require retaining an original filename, directory hierarchy, or
repository host.

5.C2 — Application to modified source

Permission to modify does not itself remove the upstream notice requirement. The
notice can identify upstream ownership without representing that all downstream
changes were written by the upstream author. This license does not separately
require marking every changed line.

5.C3 — Application to compiled copies

This license has no general exception from notice preservation for compiled copies.
Appropriate accompanying license material is a conventional way of preserving
notices, but this license does not prescribe or universally validate one
presentation for every platform.

5.C4 — Application to extracted portions

A recipient who extracts a substantial portion cannot infer exemption merely from
the fact that no original file is copied in full. Substantiality is not assigned a
fabricated numeric threshold by this license.

5.C5 — Application to small fragments

The mention of substantial portions does not warrant a conclusion that every
fragment, however trivial, is protected or subject to the same condition. Applicable
copyright law and the actual content remain relevant.

5.C6 — Application to renamed projects

A new product name does not remove upstream notices, and upstream notices do not
require retaining the old product name. A notice is not a claim of sponsorship,
certification, affiliation, or approval.

5.C7 — Application to notice collections

A larger work may present several applicable license notices together. Their
grouping does not erase the different rights and conditions that apply to separately
licensed components.

5.C8 — Application to documentation copies

Associated documentation covered by the grant is not excluded from the notice
condition merely because no program can execute it. This license covers software and
associated documentation files.

5.C9 — Application to publicity

The condition does not require a launch announcement, a social-media
acknowledgement, a testimonial, or an advertisement for the licensor. Those
activities are not substitutes for preserving the notices actually required.

5.C10 — Application to user-interface placement

This license does not expressly prescribe a splash screen, a menu label, a font
size, or a display duration. The absence of such prescriptions should not be
confused with a blanket exemption from preserving notices in binary distributions.

5.C11 — Application to license links

A link can assist a reader, but this license does not assert that a remote link by
itself always satisfies the requirement to include notices. Remote availability and
inclusion in a furnished copy are not identical facts.

5.C12 — Application to reformatting

Changes in line wrapping or presentation do not by themselves authorize omission
of an applicable upstream permission notice or disclaimer. This license does not
require the original line width. The standard MIT notice authorized in Section
5.N1 remains a sufficient alternative to reproducing this expanded document.

This preservation rule does not prohibit modifying or adapting the text as a
license template for material the adapter is entitled to license. Such adaptation
does not retrospectively change the permission applicable to material already
received under an upstream grant or dispense with notices applicable to copied
upstream material. A modified template is not represented as unmodified DamnMIT
merely because it retains the same title.

5.C13 — Application to contributor identities

A retained notice does not determine whether every contributor has been individually
named. The notice condition herein is not an exhaustive contributor-credit policy or
a solution to disputes about ownership.

5.C14 — Application to larger works

A recipient need not license its entire application under this license merely
because it includes material covered by this license. The permission to combine and
sublicense operates together with preservation of the relevant upstream notices.

5.C15 — Application to separate commercial promises

A downstream vendor can offer its own support or warranty under a separate
arrangement. Retaining the upstream disclaimer does not automatically negate the
vendor's independent promises or make the original author responsible for them.

5.C16 — Application to permission to others

The grant expressly contemplates persons to whom the Software is furnished. The
notice condition helps communicate the permission rather than requiring every
downstream recipient to contact the first licensor.

5.T1 — Meaning of "above" and "above copyright notice"

Above in the phrase above copyright notice identifies the copyright notice stated
earlier in the logical order of this instrument. It does not refer to an absolute
screen coordinate, a direction of gravity, a physical location in a room, or the
orientation of a device.

Rotating a display, using a screen reader, changing pagination, or printing in a
different layout does not independently change the identified notice. The antecedent
is determined by the document structure, not by the direction in which a viewer
happens to be facing.

5.T2 — Reordering and presentation

A reformatted presentation can place a notice on a different page or make it
reachable through an appropriate navigation structure. Such presentation does not
justify replacing the identified copyright holder with an unrelated person, deleting
applicable permission language, or describing the work as free of notice
obligations.

A malformed or incomplete presentation may make an antecedent ambiguous. This
provision does not guarantee compliance for every layout; it specifies which notice
the condition identifies and preserves the underlying requirement of inclusion.

5.T3 — Meaning of "this permission notice"

This permission notice identifies the grant and its condition expressed in this
license, including the standard MIT notice authorized in Section 5.N1. The
demonstrative this is a textual reference, not an instruction to obtain permission
from another website or to substitute an unrelated short label.

An unrelated short label, a project name, or a bare link is not the standard MIT
notice authorized in Section 5.N1. The authorization in that section concerns the
full standard MIT License with the applicable copyright notice, not an arbitrary
abbreviation of permission or an omission of its condition or disclaimer.

5.T4 — Inclusion and association

Include means retain the applicable notices in or appropriately accompanying covered
copies or substantial portions in a manner that satisfies the notice condition under
the relevant facts and applicable law. In a furnishing or distribution scenario, the
notices accompany the furnished covered material. The license does not prescribe one
universal container or technical placement.

Co-location, attachment, bundling, embedding, and accompanying documentation are
possible factual arrangements rather than guaranteed compliance certificates. A
packaging choice is evaluated as an actual presentation of the notices, not merely
as a filename chosen to sound compliant.

5.T5 — Archives and nested containers

An archive can include notices in a file within the archive, while an outer package
can include several component notices. Nesting does not itself prevent inclusion,
and the number of container layers is not a prescribed quota under this license.

This provision does not assert that a notice hidden in an inaccessible, omitted, or
unusable inner container always satisfies the condition. It also does not require a
separate identical notice in each nested layer solely because the packaging
mechanism permits arbitrary nesting.

5.T6 — Embedded notices and interfaces

A notice can be represented within a program resource or another presentation
mechanism where that arrangement satisfies the condition. This license does not
compel a particular menu name, splash screen, brand placement, font, or animation.

Embedding is not defined here as guaranteed compliance in all circumstances. The
relevant question remains whether the applicable notices are included with the
covered material as required, not whether a particular technical method was
fashionable when the work was distributed.

5.T7 — Links and remote resources

A remote link and the included notice itself are distinguishable objects.
Availability at an external address can assist interpretation but does not by this
provision establish that the actual notice has been included in every furnished
copy.

Temporary network availability, later deletion of a website, or a recipient without
network access can be relevant to the facts of presentation. No continuous-hosting
warranty is added, and no universal link-only exemption is created.

5.T8 — Inclusion is not activation

Including a notice is not the same operation as switching on a device, loading a
module, enabling an account, or initiating an installation. The possible use of the
same word in another language or technical context does not replace the textual
meaning of the notice condition.

The condition does not require a recipient to activate any service operated by the
author, send an installation notification, or request an activation key merely in
order to retain the applicable notices.

